<TopicPage slug="subjects/linguistics/foundational/how-language-works">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the opening lesson of Linguistics, and it assumes nothing. You already own the
    main laboratory equipment — you speak a language — so the lesson spends its time making
    the familiar strange enough to examine.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain the central claim of the course: that language is a complex but law-governed mental system, not a memorised phrasebook;</li>
    <li>name the layers of the system — phonemes, morphemes, syntax, semantics, pragmatics — and say what each one contributes;</li>
    <li>list the design features every language shares, spoken or signed, and use them to see why no language is "primitive";</li>
    <li>recount how writing was invented and compressed, from Sumerian receipts to the alphabet to Unicode;</li>
    <li>say how linguists know that languages are related, what a language family is, and why languages die;</li>
    <li>state the field's biggest open question: how the system gets into a child's head.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One habit: whenever a claim about language appears — "English is logical", "kids these
    days can't spell", "that dialect is broken" — we ask what the evidence from the
    <em>system</em> says. Opinions about language are cheap; observations of it are science.
  </p>

  <h2>The octopus sentence</h2>
  <p>
    There are just over 7,000 languages alive today, and every one of them does the same
    impossible job: packing infinite thoughts into a finite toolbox of sounds and rules. You
    can build a sentence nobody in history has ever uttered — "the octopus filed a complaint
    about the lift music" — and any adult speaker of English will understand it instantly,
    without effort, without instruction. That ability is the central mystery of linguistics,
    and the answer this lesson builds toward is surprising: what you are doing when you speak
    is not consulting a list of memorised sentences, but running a <em>system</em> — a complex
    but law-governed mental system. Language lives in the mind as rules and patterns, not as
    a phrasebook.
  </p>
  <p>
    The system deserves its reputation: human language is often called the most complex
    behaviour on the planet. It runs on coordinated breath, vocal cords, tongue and lips —
    or, in the world's sign languages, on hands, face and space — and it does far more than
    transmit information like "turn right at the next light". Language is how we reach other
    minds: how we draw conclusions together, set goals, negotiate who owes whom an apology,
    and hand down everything a community knows. It is also the machinery most thinking runs
    on; strip it away and human intelligence as we know it would not survive. Linguists split
    its work into <strong>receptive</strong> skills (understanding speech and text) and
    <strong>expressive</strong> ones (producing it in real time, at up to several words per
    second, with almost no conscious effort).
  </p>
  <p>
    Three facts show how deep the system runs. First, it is <strong>implicit</strong>: a
    five-year-old commands English word order flawlessly but cannot state the rule; you
    "just know" that the big red ball is fine and the red big ball is wrong, without ever
    being taught. Second, it is <strong>law-governed</strong>: the patterns are statistical
    and systematic, not loose habits — every language in the world orders its sentence
    elements in ways that follow discoverable universals. Third, it is <strong>creative</strong>:
    from a finite toolbox, an infinite range — the property Wilhelm von Humboldt called
    making "infinite use of finite means".
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> speaking is running a hidden rule system, fast and below
    awareness. To see how the trick works, we build language up layer by layer — sound,
    meaning, and the rulebook that connects them.
  </p>

  <h2>Phonemes: meaningless sound, meaningful difference</h2>
  <p>
    The bottom layer is sound, and the crucial unit is not the letter but the
    <strong>phoneme</strong> — the smallest unit of sound that can change meaning. Think of
    phonemes as the atoms of speech: individually featureless, but decisive in combination.
    Say "pat", "bat", "cat": the difference between p, b and c is all that separates three
    words, so each is a distinct phoneme of English. English runs on about 44 phonemes
    squeezed into 26 letters — hence the spelling chaos, where "shun" can be written -tion,
    -sion, -cian or just -shun.
  </p>
  <p>
    Phoneme inventories vary enormously across languages: English has about 24 consonants
    and 20 vowels; Hawaiian manages with 13 sounds in total; some Khoisan languages deploy
    dozens of click consonants. Two facts matter for the bigger picture. One: phonemes are
    themselves <em>meaningless</em> — the sound "k" means nothing alone, which is what makes
    the layer above it possible. Two: which sound differences count is language-specific —
    the breathy difference between English "p" in "pin" and "spin" is meaningful in Hindi
    but invisible to most English speakers, who literally struggle to hear it as an adult.
    Your phoneme inventory is not just what you can say; it is what you have been trained
    to <em>hear</em>.
  </p>

  <h2>Morphemes and words: the smallest pieces of meaning</h2>
  <p>
    Stack phonemes and you reach the first meaningful units: <strong>morphemes</strong> —
    the smallest pieces of a language that carry meaning. "Un-believ-abl-y" contains four:
    <em>un-</em> (not), <em>believe</em> (the root), <em>-able</em> (capable of being),
    <em>-ly</em> (in the manner of). Languages differ wildly in how much work morphemes do.
    English is fairly "analytic" — it strings separate words together ("will not have been
    going"). Latin, Turkish or Swahili are "synthetic" and pack entire clauses into one
    word: Turkish <em>evlerinizden</em> means "from your houses" in a single
    morpheme-packed unit.
  </p>
  <p>
    The vocabulary of an average educated adult runs to tens of thousands of words, yet
    almost none of them was memorised from a list — you acquired them from context, hearing
    each word a handful of times. That is itself evidence of how rule-like the acquisition
    machinery is: a lookup table that efficient does not come from storage alone.
  </p>

  <h2>Syntax and semantics: the rulebook and the meanings</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Syntax</strong> is the layer people mean by "grammar": the rules for assembling
    morphemes and words into phrases and sentences. Its defining property is
    <strong>recursion</strong> — rules that can apply to their own output. Take "the dog
    barked", embed it as a relative clause, embed that in another: "the cat that chased the
    rat that stole the cheese that sat in the house that Jack built". Nothing in principle
    stops the loop, which is how a finite rulebook generates infinite sentences. Word order
    is syntax at its most visible: English is subject–verb–object (the dog bit the man),
    Japanese is subject–object–verb, Welsh verb–subject–object — yet all three build
    questions, negations and relative clauses with the same underlying logic.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Semantics</strong> is the layer that attaches meaning, and it is compositional:
    the meaning of a sentence is built from the meanings of its parts plus the way syntax
    combines them. "The dog bit the man" and "The man bit the dog" contain identical words
    and opposite events — the syntax <em>is</em> part of the meaning. Ambiguity shows the
    layers working independently: "I saw the man with the telescope" has two readings
    because one string of words maps onto two different structures.
  </p>
  <p>
    One layer higher sits <strong>pragmatics</strong>: meaning in context. "Can you pass
    the salt?" is not a question about your physical ability, and "it's cold in here" can
    be a request to close a window. The words say one thing; the situation completes the
    meaning. Every language equips its speakers with conventions for this too — which is
    why irony, politeness and hinting survive translation only imperfectly.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "grammar" as taught in school — never split an
  infinitive, never end a sentence with a preposition — is largely 18th-century taste
  imported from Latin, not the actual rule system. The scientific position is
  <strong>descriptivist</strong>: linguists study how people <em>do</em> speak, and the
  real grammar is the implicit system every native speaker already commands. "Ain't nobody
  got time for that" is a perfectly formed sentence of a real dialect — with a negation
  system English has used for centuries. The school rules are etiquette about writing;
  the system linguists study runs in every speaker's head.</p>

  <h2>What all languages share</h2>
  <p>
    Strip away vocabulary and every language, spoken or signed, shows the same design
    features (the linguist Charles Hockett's classic list, distilled):
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Productivity.</strong> You have never heard most sentences you can instantly understand or build. No animal communication system does this — bee dances and vervet alarm calls are fixed inventories, not generative systems.</li>
    <li><strong>Displacement.</strong> We talk about yesterday, tomorrow, and things that don't exist. Language escapes the here-and-now.</li>
    <li><strong>Duality of patterning.</strong> A small inventory of meaningless sounds combines into vast meaningful vocabularies — the phoneme-and-morpheme trick from the last two sections, which is what makes writing, and then typing, possible.</li>
    <li><strong>Arbitrariness.</strong> The link between sound and meaning is conventional — "dog", "chien", "perro", "inu". (Onomatopoeia is the tiny exception that proves the rule.)</li>
    <li><strong>Cultural transmission and learnability.</strong> No child is born speaking Khmer or Quechua; any infant raised in any language community acquires that language on roughly the same schedule — including deaf children acquiring sign languages, which are full languages with their own phonology and syntax, not gestures.</li>
  </ul>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "some languages are primitive, and modern ones are
  more evolved." It seems plausible because technologies have histories of improvement —
  but a language is not a machine, it is a system every child masters completely, and no
  language ever documented has lacked anything essential. Every one has full syntax, a
  rich phonology and expressive range, and the languages of small communities often pack
  more grammar into each word than English does. All 7,000 are running the same design,
  at the same specification, for the same jobs.</p>

  <h2>Writing: five millennia of freezing speech</h2>
  <p>
    Speech is maybe 100,000+ years old; writing is about five. When it was finally invented
    — apparently independently in Mesopotamia, Egypt, China and Mesoamerica — it began not
    with poetry but with accounting: Sumerian clay tablets recording grain and sheep,
    because remembered debts are deniable debts.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "the written form is the real language and speech is
  its sloppy version." The arrow runs the other way: speech came first by roughly 100,000
  years, is acquired without instruction, and carries the primary system; writing is a
  late, optional technology that most languages never adopted. Linguists therefore treat
  transcripts of real speech — hesitations, repairs and all — as the data, not as
  corrupted text.</p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="five millennia of writing — from Sumerian receipts to a script for every language" widget="timeline" config={writeTimeline} />

  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> each step on the timeline is a compression of the
    sound–sign mapping. Pictograms draw things; word-signs stand for words; the rebus trick
    borrows signs for their sound alone ("I ♥ you"); the alphabet takes the final step of
    one sign per phoneme — a couple of dozen symbols instead of hundreds. Watch the tags
    change from "invention" to "the alphabet" to "the machine": after the Greek addition of
    vowels made writing a complete transcription of speech, the remaining milestones are
    about copying and encoding, not about the principle. The 1991 Unicode milestone
    completes the story: one standard encoding every script on Earth, so the writing system
    is finally independent of any single language or machine.
  </p>
  <p>
    Writing systems themselves sort into a spectrum, and most real scripts are hybrids.
    <strong>Logographic</strong> systems (Chinese characters) map signs to words or
    morphemes — thousands to learn, but compact and readable across dialects and even
    across mutually unintelligible spoken languages. <strong>Syllabic</strong> systems
    (Japanese kana, Linear B) map signs to syllables — dozens to hundreds of symbols.
    <strong>Alphabetic</strong> systems map signs to phonemes — the cheapest to learn, as
    the timeline showed. Japanese illustrates the hybrid strategy: kanji carry the content
    words while kana carry the grammar, a division of labour between system types inside
    one sentence. The choice of system is history, not quality: Korean moved from Chinese
    characters to its featural hangul alphabet (1443, consciously designed), while Chinese
    stayed logographic for cultural continuity — both work; they price literacy
    differently.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the layers of language — sound, meaning-carrying units,
    syntax, meaning, context — recur in every language, with the same design features, and
    writing is a five-millennia-old technology for freezing the spoken original. Now: where
    all these languages came from, and where they are going.
  </p>

  <h2>Families, change and death</h2>
  <p>
    Languages are never still. Pronunciation drifts, meanings shift ("nice" once meant
    foolish), and populations split — and over centuries the drift compounds into
    dialects, then separate languages. English, Hindi, Spanish, Bengali, Russian and Greek
    are all cousins in the <strong>Indo-European family</strong>, descended from a common
    ancestor spoken roughly 5,500 years ago on the Pontic steppe; about 46% of humanity
    speaks its descendant languages today.
  </p>
  <p>
    How do we know the family tree is real and not a guess? Because the correspondences
    are <em>systematic</em>, not accidental. Grimm's law (1822) showed Latin <em>p</em>
    consistently corresponds to English <em>f</em> (pater/father, piscis/fish, ped/foot) —
    a regular sound change, not a coincidence. Rules like this run through whole
    vocabularies, and they are what let linguists reconstruct dead ancestor languages like
    Proto-Indo-European from living descendants, the way astronomers infer an unseen body
    from its pull on visible ones.
  </p>
  <p>
    The family tree is now pruning itself. Roughly half of today's 7,000 languages have
    fewer than about 7,000 speakers and are endangered; a language dies roughly every few
    weeks. Every loss takes with it an untranslatable archive — ecological knowledge, oral
    history, a way of carving up the world. Revitalisation efforts (Welsh, Māori, Hawaiian
    immersion schools) show decline is not inevitable; it follows prestige and policy, and
    both can be changed.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>"The red big ball" sounds wrong to native speakers who were never taught any rule. What does that tell you about where grammar lives?</li>
    <li>How many morphemes are in "unhappiness", and what does each contribute?</li>
    <li>Why does the existence of duality of patterning make writing possible?</li>
    <li>Latin <em>pater</em>, English <em>father</em>, German <em>Vater</em>. What kind of evidence is this for a language family — and why does a single word pair prove nothing?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Grammar is an implicit system in the mind, not a set of
    memorised rules from school — the adjective-order rule was never taught, yet every
    native speaker obeys it. (2) Three: <em>un-</em> (not), <em>happy</em> (the root), and
    <em>-ness</em> (turning an adjective into a noun). Each piece contributes a distinct
    meaning; none can be split further without losing meaning. (3) If a small inventory of
    meaningless units combines into unlimited meaningful ones, you only need to invent
    symbols for the small inventory — a couple of dozen signs can then write anything. A
    system with no such layer would need a symbol per meaning, which is the logographic
    burden. (4) Systematic sound correspondences across many words — one regular
    correspondence (p/f) repeated across the vocabulary is evidence of common descent,
    while one word pair could be borrowing, chance or onomatopoeia. The power is in the
    pattern, not the instance.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Language is a complex but law-governed mental system: implicit (speakers cannot state its rules), systematic (patterns follow discoverable universals) and creative (infinite use of finite means).</li>
    <li>Its layers: phonemes (meaningless sound that makes a difference), morphemes (smallest units of meaning), syntax (the recursive rulebook), semantics (compositional meaning), pragmatics (meaning in context).</li>
    <li>Every language — spoken or signed — shares productivity, displacement, duality of patterning, arbitrariness and cultural transmission; none is primitive.</li>
    <li>Speech is primary; writing is a late, optional technology whose history is a march of compression toward one-sign-per-phoneme.</li>
    <li>Systematic sound correspondences reveal language families (Indo-European alone covers about 46% of humanity) and let linguists reconstruct lost ancestors.</li>
    <li>About half the world's languages are endangered; decline follows prestige and policy, and both can change.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    This lesson sketched the whole machine in one pass; the rest of the course zooms into
    its parts. Upcoming lessons look closer at how children acquire the system, at how
    sounds are actually produced and perceived, at how meaning and context interact, and
    at how languages change on measurable timescales. Keep this page in mind as the map:
    every later lesson is one region of it, drawn at higher magnification.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The deepest question in the field is <em>how the system gets into the child's head</em>.
    Children say things they have never heard ("goed", "two foots") — evidence they are
    applying rules, not copying input. The poverty-of-the-stimulus argument holds that the
    data a child hears is too sparse to explain the grammar they converge on, so the mind
    must bring strong built-in expectations (Chomsky's Universal Grammar). Critics respond
    that statistical learning from rich input, plus general cognition, can do the job; the
    debate — nature versus nurture for grammar — remains genuinely open, with modern
    usage-based accounts and generative accounts still fighting it out.
  </p>
  <p>
    Other expert edges: <strong>pidgins and creoles</strong> — when adult speakers with no
    common language improvise a bare contact pidgin, their children convert it into a full
    creole with regular grammar within one generation, the closest thing nature offers to
    a grammar-creation experiment. <strong>Sign languages</strong> prove the sound layer is
    not essential: they have phonemes (handshape, location, movement) and duality of
    patterning with no sound at all — American Sign Language alone serves hundreds of
    thousands of people in the United States, and it is a separate language from English,
    not a transcription of it. <strong>Writing is not language</strong>: speech is primary
    biologically; literacy is a cultural technology bolted on, which is why dyslexia can
    impair reading while leaving spoken language intact. And the origin question — how
    recursion and displacement first evolved — remains one of the great open problems,
    constrained but unanswered by primate communication, fossil anatomy and genetics
    (FOXP2 among other genes).
  </p>

